1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,775
What if hearing a lie repeatedly

2
00:00:01,788 --> 00:00:03,250
makes it feel true?

3
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:04,343
The first time you hear a

4
00:00:04,355 --> 00:00:06,106
claim, you might doubt it.

5
00:00:06,136 --> 00:00:07,119
The second time,

6
00:00:07,149 --> 00:00:08,875
it sounds a little familiar.

7
00:00:08,905 --> 00:00:10,100
By the hundredth time,

8
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:11,518
your brain processes it more

9
00:00:11,530 --> 00:00:12,283
easily.

10
00:00:12,313 --> 00:00:14,200
And that easy, familiar feeling

11
00:00:14,213 --> 00:00:16,297
can be mistaken for truth.

12
00:00:16,327 --> 00:00:17,714
Psychologists call this the

13
00:00:17,727 --> 00:00:19,521
Illusory Truth Effect.

14
00:00:19,551 --> 00:00:21,364
It's one reason repetition is so

15
00:00:21,376 --> 00:00:23,089
powerful in advertising and

16
00:00:23,101 --> 00:00:24,337
misinformation.

17
00:00:24,367 --> 00:00:25,817
Research suggests it can even

18
00:00:25,830 --> 00:00:27,192
affect people who already know

19
00:00:27,205 --> 00:00:28,730
the correct answer.

20
00:00:28,847 --> 00:00:30,184
So next time something feels

21
00:00:30,197 --> 00:00:32,222
true, ask yourself why.

22
00:00:32,252 --> 00:00:34,317
Remember, familiarity is not

23
00:00:34,331 --> 00:00:35,325
proof.
